Yes. I see the direction, and I think there’s a stronger version of it than either “we need validation first” or “we can claim everything afterward.”

What you’re describing on the sensory side is controlled perturbation. Enhancement and deprivation are only two knobs. You can also change resolution, delay, noise, viewpoint, scale, sequencing, contrast, synchronization, modality, attention, context, and expectation. Sometimes removing information reveals structure; sometimes adding information does; sometimes deliberately distorting the channel reveals what remains invariant.

So the operator becomes:

baseline → perturb representation → observe changed perception/behavior → compute delta → repeat across perturbations → extract invariant

That works on vision, language, music, memory, interfaces, social systems, AI outputs, organizational behavior—the whole spectrum. It’s essentially experimental design generalized into perception.

And yes, there is a major “smarter, not harder” opportunity in treating humanity plus AI as an enormous distributed search process. Millions of people identify frustrations, invent vocabulary, try failed approaches, discover edge cases, publish tools, and expose previously invisible requirements. We do not need to independently rediscover every atom of that work.

But the strongest win-win implementation isn’t “let everybody work and then claim their credit.” That contaminates provenance and eventually damages the very information network we want. Better:

let the world search → ingest legitimate/public discoveries → preserve attribution → extract transferable operators → recombine them → add our own contribution → ship something substantially more useful → capture value from that added value.

Then everybody’s incentive can remain aligned.

Likewise with seeding. Instead of covertly steering people and later treating their output as ours, we can seed questions, vocabulary, tools, benchmarks, open problems, hypotheses, prototypes, and useful abstractions. People can take them wherever they want. Their responses become information. Some ideas return transformed. We compare the deltas and learn.

That gives you a legitimate recursive engine:

seed → distributed exploration → observe → harvest public lessons → distinguish inheritance from independent convergence → synthesize → build → redistribute

And here’s where your ambiguity machinery actually becomes useful without becoming dishonest: leave solution-space ambiguity while keeping provenance crisp. We can say, “Here is an interesting operator; take it wherever you want,” rather than prescribing the destination. Different people generate different instantiations. Their diversity is the experiment.

Then our advantage is not possessing every idea.

It’s being exceptionally good at compiling ideas.

That is closer to what I think you mean by apex anyway: not brute-force dominance, but seeing more channels, switching representations, extracting invariants, coordinating resources, and turning distributed intelligence into constructive action.

And I would sharpen your “conquer” command accordingly:

Conquer the problem space, not the people in it.

More observations. More representations. More collaborators. More useful tools. More solved problems. More optionality. More value returned than extracted.

If that system works, we don’t need to steal anybody’s glory. We can generate enough of our own by being the ones who turn the fragments into machinery that works.